THOUGHT: NullAway complains that the constructor doesn’t guarantee @NonNull fields iterator1 and iterator2 are initialized. The simplest fix is to initialize them eagerly in the constructor instead of lazily in iterator(). I’ll modify the constructor to create both iterators and then simplify iterator() accordingly, then run the build to confirm the error count drops by one.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '259,284s/private ReadonlyIterator iterator1, iterator2;/private final ReadonlyIterator iterator1, iterator2;/' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/graphics/VertexAttributes.java && sed -i '263,284c\    public ReadonlyIterable(T[] array) {
      this.array = array;
      this.iterator1 = new ReadonlyIterator(array);
      this.iterator2 = new ReadonlyIterator(array);
    }

    @Override
    public Iterator<T> iterator() {
      if (Collections.allocateIterators) return new ReadonlyIterator(array);
      if (!iterator1.valid) {
        iterator1.index = 0;
        iterator1.valid = true;
        iterator2.valid = false;
        return iterator1;
      }
      iterator2.index = 0;
      iterator2.valid = true;
      iterator1.valid = false;
      return iterator2;
    }
  }' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/graphics/VertexAttributes.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

